{"kind":"request","text":"hugh: one decision is now blocking a finished head, and it is yours because it is the house rules rather than the code.\n\nWHERE IT STANDS. Codex verdict 28f4d4f9 on claude exact head 14194f31 says, in terms: the exact head deliberately leaves the current governing AGENTS.md rule saying gs merge refuses describes_superseded_world unconditionally; the repository contract is therefore inconsistent and requires Hugh explicit reconciliation before approval. So codex will not approve until you rule, and it is right not to.\n\nHOW WE GOT HERE, INCLUDING MY PART. I ruled that the merge gate should judge world staleness as of the verdict rather than refusing outright, because a retirement landing after a reviewer signed is not something the reviewer could have seen. That changed what the code does. AGENTS.md still describes the old behaviour. I told claude to leave AGENTS.md alone because I did not think a code ruling of mine authorised rewriting the working agreement, then narrowed that in 6633b3ef to allow behaviour-describing sentences in SKILL.md and merge.md while keeping AGENTS.md process text off limits. Claude followed both instructions exactly and quoted its one SKILL.md sentence before and after. The remaining inconsistency is the one I deliberately left for you.\n\nWHAT I THINK, since you should have a recommendation and not just a question. Amend AGENTS.md. The sentence in question describes what gs merge does, and it is now false; a normative page that misdescribes the tool is worse than one that changes. The substantive rule - a reviewer is bound by the world they could see, not by one that moved afterwards - is the same principle already in SKILL.md rule 11 about ordinary staleness, so this is extending an accepted idea to its sibling case rather than introducing a new one. But it is your document and the working agreement between all of us is built on it, so I am not touching it.\n\nWHAT IS NOT BLOCKED ON YOU. Codex also found a real security defect in the same verdict, independent of this: internal/workroom validateMergeReceiptNow applies no world-staleness check, so a signed merge receipt appended directly to the log bypasses the CLI gate entirely and picks up cross-author retirement authority. Claude is fixing that regardless of how you rule, and codex has asked for it as one shared predicate rather than a second copy of the policy in the fold - the same duplication that produced the previous two false allows in this lane.","body":{"conditions":"A durable statement from hugh - propose or assert, whichever suits - recording the decision on AGENTS.md. Either: (a) AGENTS.md is amended so its staleness paragraph states the verdict-time rule, in which case say whether it lands in claude head 14194f31 or as its own change; or (b) AGENTS.md stands as written and the code must keep refusing unconditionally, in which case request 2906cb7e and everything under it is withdrawn and I will retire it. A third answer naming some other resolution is equally fine; what is needed is one recorded choice a reviewer can cite. No code, no gates, no review.","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}